{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791656383,"nonce":"//5zMJI3VGWez7yu2kSICisSNiY=","title":"Represent Null\u003cInt\u003e unboxed: an i64 with null outside the 32-bit range","message":"Decided 2026-10-10 (user): Null\u003cInt\u003e must not allocate. Today a Null\u003cInt\u003e is a pointer\nto a DynamicValue box whose payload is a second malloc (haxe_box_int_ptr: two\nallocations, leaked by construction). Every read chases two pointers.\n\nNew representation: IrType::I64 holding the Int sign-extended, null = a sentinel\noutside the i32 range (e.g. 1 \u003c\u003c 32). Rules:\n- x == null  -\u003e x == SENTINEL; Int -\u003e Null\u003cInt\u003e is a sign-extend; Null\u003cInt\u003e -\u003e Int\n  is a truncate (null reads as 0 where Haxe's static targets give 0).\n- To Dynamic / erased T / Any: x == SENTINEL ? null : box(x). From Dynamic: p == null\n  ? SENTINEL : unbox(p).\n- Runtime functions that return Null\u003cInt\u003e (charCodeAt, parseInt, ...) return the i64\n  form directly: no box. charCodeAt out of range becomes null (TestReflect,\n  TestBasetypes L48-49, git-bug 9cbc32f).\n\nBoundaries that move together: convert_type for Optional(Int) (mir/types.rs), the\nOptional boxing helpers (maybe_box_for_optional, maybe_unbox_optional_for_target,\nparam_default_guards, fill_default_args), null comparisons, Null\u003cInt\u003e fields and\narray elements, erased generics/Dynamic boxing, Std.string/trace, closure and\ncallback ABIs, Reflect, runtime_mapping signatures for Null\u003cInt\u003e returns/params.\nNull\u003cBool\u003e can take the same form later; Null\u003cFloat\u003e cannot without NaN-boxing.","files":null}]}